                </td>
                <td width=572 valign=center colspan=10 style="width:343.6000pt;padding:0.0000pt 5.4000pt 0.0000pt 5.4000pt ;border-left:none;
mso-border-left-alt:none;border-right:1.0000pt solid windowtext;mso-border-right-alt:0.5000pt solid windowtext;
border-top:none;mso-border-top-alt:0.5000pt solid windowtext;border-bottom:1.0000pt solid windowtext;
mso-border-bottom-alt:0.5000pt solid windowtext;">
                    <p class=MsoNormal><span style="font-family:仿宋;mso-bidi-font-family:'Times New Roman';font-size:12.0000pt;
mso-font-kerning:1.0000pt;"><font face="仿宋"><div>
<div>As a landmark breakthrough in modern biomedical research, tumor immune checkpoint therapy has brought revolutionary changes to cancer diagnosis and treatment and become a global research hotspot. This field offers extensive development potential in novel target discovery, combinatorial target design, antibody-drug conjugate development, and combination immunotherapy. Mastering the cutting-edge theories and research paradigms in this field is essential for training top-tier pharmaceutical graduate students to understand biomedical research rules and strengthen their capacity for new drug innovation and clinical translation. This course, tailored for the training of outstanding pharmaceutical innovative talents, systematically elaborates the molecular mechanisms, regulatory networks, drug resistance mechanisms, and clinical translation strategies of classic immune checkpoints including CTLA-4 and PD-1/PD-L1. It also presents the latest research progress of novel immune checkpoints and tumor combination immunotherapy. Combined with the development history and translational logic of tumor immune drugs, this course enables students to build a comprehensive knowledge system of tumor immunotherapy and cultivates their professional scientific thinking and innovative translational competence. This course is based on the textbook <em>Regulation of Cancer Immune Checkpoints</em>.</div>
</div></font></span><span style="font-family:'Times New Roman';mso-fareast-font-family:仿宋;font-size:10.5000pt;
mso-font-kerning:1.0000pt;"><o:p></o:p></span></p>
                </td>
            </tr>
